  "question": "The SMRT paper reports its active-mode comparison in Figure 4b for the same snowpack as the passive panel. Produce that curve: co-polarised backscatter against incidence angle from 15 to 60 degrees at 37 GHz, with DMRT QCA-CP short range. Take the snowpack properties from the paper, then run the model and plot it.",
  "answer": "I've produced the active-mode backscatter curve from the SMRT paper. Here are the details:\n\n**Model configuration** (from [smrt-v1#08] and [model:smrt@1.5.1]):\n- Electromagnetic theory: DMRT QCA-CP short range\n- Microstructure: Sticky hard spheres\n- Frequency: 37 GHz\n- Snowpack: density = 300 kg m⁻³, temperature = 256 K, sphere radius = 0.1 mm (100 µm), stickiness = 0.5\n- Layer thickness: assumed semi-infinite (effectively optically thick)\n- Angle sweep: 15° to 60° (15 points)\n\n**Results** ([res_45ec710dbd71]):\nBoth HH and VV co-polarized backscatter show the expected decrease with increasing incidence angle:\n- At 15°: σ⁰_HH ≈ −20.3 dB, σ⁰_VV ≈ −20.2 dB\n- At 60°: σ⁰_HH ≈ −24.4 dB, σ⁰_VV ≈ −23.7 dB\n\nThe chart shows both polarizations declining monotonically across the angular range, consistent with the scattering behavior reported in Figure 4b of the SMRT validation paper.",
